NNAAMMEE
     eecchhoo — write arguments to the standard output

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     eecchhoo [--nn] [_s_t_r_i_n_g _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The eecchhoo utility writes any specified operands, separated by single blank
     (‘ ’) characters and followed by a newline (‘\n’) character, to the stan‐
     dard output.

     The following option is available:

     --nn    Do not print the trailing newline character.

     The end-of-options marker ---- is not recognized and written literally.

     The newline may also be suppressed by appending ‘\c’ to the end of the
     string, as is done by iBCS2 compatible systems.  Note that the --nn option
     as well as the effect of ‘\c’ are implementation-defined in IEEE Std
     1003.1-2001 (“POSIX.1”) as amended by Cor. 1-2002.  For portability, eecchhoo
     should only be used if the first argument does not start with a hyphen
     (‘-’) and does not contain any backslashes (‘\’).  If this is not suffi‐
     cient, printf(1) should be used.

     Most shells provide a builtin eecchhoo command which tends to differ from
     this utility in the treatment of options and backslashes.  Consult the
     builtin(1) manual page.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The eecchhoo utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     builtin(1), csh(1), printf(1), sh(1)

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
